  "key_ideas": [
    "A year of agent tooling was built on the assumption that models need heavy scaffolding: prompt chains, hardcoded data filters, manual memory/RAG pipelines.",
    "Anthropic's own benchmarks flipped that assumption: Claude 4 hits near 50% on SWE-bench using only bash and a text editor \u2014 no custom orchestration.",
    "On BrowseComp, accuracy jumped from 45.3% to 61.6% when the model wrote its own code filters instead of using hand-built ones.",
    "The new pitch: code is the orchestration language. Give the model a sandboxed shell and a REPL and let it write its own glue.",
    "On April 4, Anthropic cut third-party harnesses (OpenClaw, Hermes, and similar Claude wrappers) off subscription billing \u2014 they now need separate pay-as-you-go usage.",
    "Claude Code and Claude Cowork stay covered; everything built outside Anthropic's sanctioned surface area got metered overnight.",
    "The stated enterprise tension: maximum model performance wants maximum autonomy, but compliance wants control \u2014 the proposed fix is infrastructure (sandboxing, audit trails, permissioning), not a return to rigid harnesses.",
    "This mirrors a pattern enterprise engineers have lived through before: years spent building abstraction layers over a platform, only to watch the platform absorb that exact capability."
  ],
  "scenes": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "name": "Hook",
      "script": "Anthropic just told every team building AI agents to delete half their code. Not as a joke, not as a hot take \u2014 as the literal architecture recommendation in their own engineering guidance. For the last year, I watched teams, including ones I've worked with, pour months into orchestration layers around these models. Prompt chains. Hardcoded filters. Custom memory pipelines. All of it built on one assumption: that the model needed us to hold its hand. That assumption just broke. And on the same week, Anthropic quietly cut off a huge chunk of the tools that were built on top of it. I want to walk you through both stories, because if you're building anything with AI agents right now, this changes what you should be building next.",
      "words": 128,
      "card_script": "Here's the shift in one frame: a year of heavy scaffolding, replaced almost overnight by a much simpler idea.",
