    {
      "id": 2,
      "name": "The Assumption Everyone Made",
      "script": "Here's the assumption that shaped almost every agent framework over the past year: models are unreliable narrators, so you wrap them in structure. You chain prompts so each step only sees what it needs. You hardcode filters so the model never touches raw, messy data. You build memory systems and retrieval pipelines so the model doesn't have to reason over everything itself. I've built versions of this in enterprise systems for years \u2014 not for AI, for plain old software. Every time a platform felt unreliable, the answer was always the same: add a layer. Add a queue. Add a cache. Add a translation service in between. It felt like good engineering. It was good engineering, for the platforms we had at the time.",
      "words": 124,
      "card_script": "The old playbook, in three layers: prompt chains, hardcoded filters, and manual memory pipelines.",
