    {
      "id": 3,
      "name": "The Number That Changes Everything",
      "script": "Then Anthropic published the numbers that undercut the whole premise. Claude 4, given nothing but a sandboxed bash shell and a text editor, hit close to fifty percent on SWE-bench. No custom orchestration. No bespoke tool design. Just the raw model and two generic tools it could use however it wanted. And on a benchmark called BrowseComp, accuracy jumped from forty-five point three percent to sixty-one point six percent the moment the model was allowed to write its own filtering code instead of using filters engineers had hand-built for it. Read that twice. The model did better when we stopped pre-deciding how it should think. That's not a small tooling update. That's a different relationship between engineer and model.",
      "words": 119,
      "card_script": "Forty-five point three percent to sixty-one point six. The model wrote its own filters and got better.",
