    {
      "id": 5,
      "name": "What This Means For Engineers",
      "script": "Here's what I keep coming back to from my own years in enterprise software: the abstraction layer you build to compensate for a weak platform becomes technical debt the moment the platform gets strong. I've shipped middleware whose entire job was translating between two systems that, two years later, learned to talk to each other directly. The middleware didn't fail. It just became unnecessary weight nobody wanted to own. That's exactly what's happening to a lot of agent scaffolding right now. If your harness exists because the model couldn't reason over messy data, that reason is disappearing. The engineering question isn't 'how do I build a better harness.' It's 'which parts of my harness am I keeping out of habit, not necessity.'",
      "words": 122,
      "card_script": "The harness you built to cover a weak model becomes debt the moment the model gets strong.",
      "video_id": "7350af1777ce4d0b9572cfe9c819fa31",
      "status": "completed",
      "card_video_id": "80f56d334c3143479929d120a8e12aef",
